---
// perclient_1110 M3.3 — use case: one private bundle per client.
// Every number on this page is pinned by tests/perclient-1110-usecase.test.ts
// to the measured reproduction (M1-transcript.md, committed as
// tests/fixtures/perclient-1110-allowlist.txt). Add a number here only after
// it has been measured and added to that allowlist.
import AppShell from '../../layouts/AppShell.astro';
---

<AppShell mode="public"
  title="One private skill bundle per client, on every machine"
  description="Keep each client's agent skills in their own private bundle and install it identically on every machine. Reproduced on Debian x86_64 and macOS arm64 on 2026-10-11, with the exact commands and when not to use it."
>
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-16 pb-24">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Use case</p>
    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-semibold tracking-tight mb-6">
      One private skill bundle per client, the same on every machine
    </h1>
    <p class="text-muted text-lg leading-relaxed mb-12">
      You run coding agents for several clients, on more than one machine. Each client has its own
      conventions, so you keep per-client skills, and the copies drift apart. Below: what other people
      report, who this setup is (and is not) for, what we reproduced on two machines on 2026-10-11, and
      the exact text to hand your agent.
    </p>

    <!-- What they report -->
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-2">What people are doing — what they report</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-soft mb-6">Their words, quoted from their own posts. These are their reports, not our measurements.</p>
    <div class="space-y-5 mb-6">
      <figure class="border-l-2 border-accent pl-4">
        <blockquote class="text-text leading-relaxed">“Across two Claude accounts and a couple of machines it stops being manageable: every install is per profile and per box”</blockquote>
        <figcaption class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">— Christopher Penkin,
          <a href="https://www.penkin.me/ai/development/tools/dotfiles/2026/07/30/syncing-claude-skills-across-machines.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-accent hover:opacity-80 underline">penkin.me</a>, 2026-07-30</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="border-l-2 border-accent pl-4">
        <blockquote class="text-text leading-relaxed">“Or I improve a project skill on my MacBook and later discover that the Linux box still has the old version.”</blockquote>
        <figcaption class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">— Maksym Prokopov,
          <a href="https://prokopov.me/posts/skillsync-reusing-ai-agent-skills/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-accent hover:opacity-80 underline">prokopov.me</a>, 2026-05-11</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="border-l-2 border-accent pl-4">
        <blockquote class="text-text leading-relaxed">“you write a skill for one workflow, it works great, and then you need something almost identical for another client. So you copy it.”</blockquote>
        <figcaption class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">— MindStudio blog,
          <a href="https://www.mindstudio.ai/blog/modular-skill-system-claude-code-multi-client-2" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-accent hover:opacity-80 underline">mindstudio.ai</a>, 2026-05-25</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p class="text-muted mb-12">
      Prokopov solved it with a synced folder and a small local tool, with no cloud service. Penkin uses a public
      registry plus a daily sync script. Both are reasonable answers.
    </p>

    <!-- Who this is for -->
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-4">Who this is for, and who it is not for</h2>
    <div class="space-y-4 mb-12">
      <div class="bg-bg-card border border-border rounded-xl p-5">
        <p class="text-text font-semibold mb-1">Comfortable with git?</p>
        <p class="text-muted">
          A private dotfiles repo plus symlinks, or a free open-source tool such as claude-sync, gaal or
          chezmoi, solves cross-machine sync at no cost. If that already works for you, keep it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-bg-card border border-border rounded-xl p-5">
        <p class="text-text font-semibold mb-1">Client material is not confidential?</p>
        <p class="text-muted">
          Use a public bundle. Public bundles are free and unlimited on every tier, and nothing on this page
          costs you anything.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-bg-card border border-border rounded-xl p-5">
        <p class="text-text font-semibold mb-1">Client material is secret?</p>
        <p class="text-muted">
          Do not put it on anyone's hosted service, ours included. Self-host LoopSkill instead: it is free and
          MPL-2.0, so the bundles live on a server you run
          (<a href="https://github.com/wisechef-ai/loopskill-api/blob/main/docs/SELF_HOST.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-accent hover:opacity-80 underline">self-host guide</a>).
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-bg-card border border-accent rounded-xl p-5">
        <p class="text-text font-semibold mb-1">Private but not secret, on 2 or more machines?</p>
        <p class="text-muted">
          This is what hosted private bundles are for: coding conventions, brand voice, runbooks. What you
          get over a repo: no repo to maintain, one bundle per client kept separate,
          tarballs downloaded through signed URLs, each checked by its sha256, one install command per machine,
          and on Pro, separate API keys you can revoke per machine or per client. You do not run a server.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- What we reproduced -->
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-2">What we reproduced</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-soft mb-4">Our own measurement, 2026-10-11. One Free account, one API key, used on both machines.</p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-muted mb-4">
      <li><strong class="text-text">Two machines.</strong> A: Debian 13, x86_64, Python 3.12. B: macOS 27, arm64 (Apple silicon), Python 3.9 with the standard library only.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-text">One bundle, installed on both.</strong> A client bundle with 3 skills: 1.28 s on A, 1.33 s on B. The folder fingerprint (sha256 over every file) was <code class="text-xs bg-bg-elev rounded px-1">72d57592…</code> on both machines: identical.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-text">Change on one machine, update the other with one command.</strong> We added a skill to the bundle from A, then re-ran the install on B: 3 kept, 1 added, 0.97 s, so about a second. Fingerprint <code class="text-xs bg-bg-elev rounded px-1">169062a9…</code> on both: identical again.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-text">One API key on both machines at once.</strong> The same key ran on A and B at the same time: 6 of 6 concurrent calls OK. You do not need a key per machine.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-text">Remove leaves nothing.</strong> On B we deleted the 4 skill folders and the lockfile; nothing was left in the skills folder.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-soft mb-12">
      What did not work as documented, filed as issues: our agent docs do not describe the private-bundle lifecycle yet
      (loopskill-api#397); adding a skill to a bundle is a REST call with no MCP tool yet; the Liked bundle takes a
      private slot (loopskill-api#398, see below); <code class="text-xs bg-bg-elev rounded px-1">loopskill_sync</code>
      moves the bundle's pinned versions on the server and does not update a machine (loopskill-api#400).
    </p>

    <!-- Send this to your agent -->
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-4">Send this to your agent</h2>
    <p class="text-text font-semibold mb-2">Step 1 — you, in a browser</p>
    <p class="text-muted mb-3">
      <a href="/signin?next=/library&utm_source=usecase&utm_medium=page&utm_campaign=uc-per-client" class="text-accent hover:opacity-80 underline">Sign in</a>.
      Your Library shows the API key we generated when you signed up, once: copy it from there. If you already
      closed that reveal, create or revoke keys on your Account page. Then give that key to your agent.
    </p>
    <p class="text-muted mb-6">
      Why you and not the agent: an agent can register its own key, but an agent-registered key is denied checkout
      by design, so that account can never be upgraded.
    </p>

    <p class="text-text font-semibold mb-2">Step 2 — paste this into your agent</p>
    <div class="relative mb-12">
      <button type="button" data-copy-prompt class="absolute top-2 right-2 text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded-md border border-border text-muted hover:text-text bg-bg-card">Copy</button>
      <pre data-agent-prompt class="bg-[#0d0d0d] border border-border rounded-xl p-4 pr-16 text-sm text-text overflow-x-auto whitespace-pre-wrap"><code>Set up a private LoopSkill bundle for one client and install it on this machine. Use the API key I give you; do not register a new account or key yourself. Talk to the LoopSkill MCP endpoint https://app.loopskill.io/api/mcp/http/ and send the header x-api-key: &lt;MY_KEY&gt; on every call.
1. Once, on the first machine only: call loopskill_compose_bundle_from_links with &#123;"links": ["skill://&lt;slug&gt;", ...], "name": "&lt;client-name&gt;"&#125; and note the bundle id it returns; tell me the id. Never call loopskill_compose_bundle_from_links again on another machine: every call creates a new bundle, and on Free a second one can hit the private-bundle cap. If I give you a bundle id, skip this step.
2. On every machine, including the first: call loopskill_bundle_install with &#123;"bundle_id": "&lt;id&gt;"&#125;. For each skill it returns a tarball URL and a sha256. Download each tarball, check the sha256 of the downloaded bytes and stop on a mismatch, unpack it into ~/.claude/skills/&lt;slug&gt;/ (or this agent's skills folder), and keep a lockfile with slug, version and sha256.
3. To update a machine after the bundle changes, run step 2 again on that machine.
4. To add a skill: POST https://app.loopskill.io/api/bundles/&lt;id&gt;/skills with the x-api-key header and the body &#123;"slug": "&lt;slug&gt;"&#125; (REST; there is no MCP tool for this yet), then run step 2 again on each machine.
Note: loopskill_sync does not update this machine. It only moves the bundle's pinned versions on the server. Re-running step 2 is what updates the machine.</code></pre>
    </div>

    <!-- At the cap -->
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-4">At the cap</h2>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      Free includes 2 private bundles and 1 API key. The Liked bundle is created the first time you open your Library,
      and it is private, so it uses one of the 2 Free slots: in practice that leaves one client bundle. The next one
      is refused with this message:
    </p>
    <pre class="bg-[#0d0d0d] border border-border rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-text overflow-x-auto whitespace-pre-wrap mb-4"><code>private bundle limit reached for tier 'free' (2/2). Publishing a bundle frees its slot (public bundles are unlimited); or upgrade to compose more.</code></pre>
    <p class="text-muted mb-6">
      Publishing a client bundle does free its slot, but then that bundle is public. Only do that with material you
      would show anyone.
    </p>
    <div class="bg-bg-card border border-border rounded-xl p-5 mb-4">
      <p class="text-text font-semibold text-lg mb-2">Pro $9.95/mo: 50 private bundles, 10 API keys</p>
      <p class="text-muted mb-4">
        One key works on every machine; we ran the same key on both machines at the same time. The 10 keys are for
        revoking one machine or one client separately, not a requirement.
      </p>
      <a href="/signin?next=/api/checkout/pro&utm_source=usecase&utm_medium=page&utm_campaign=uc-per-client" class="inline-block px-5 py-3 rounded-lg font-semibold text-sm bg-accent text-bg hover:bg-accent-soft transition">Upgrade to Pro</a>
    </div>
    <p class="text-text font-semibold mb-2">One client bundle is enough (your Liked list takes the other Free slot), and you only put catalog skills in it? Stay on Free.</p>
    <p class="text-muted mb-12">
      A few little-used hosted endpoints (skill forks, draft-to-skill, sync streaming and some others) also require Pro; don't buy it for those, they may be retired.
    </p>

    <p class="text-sm text-muted-soft">
      See also: <a href="/pricing" class="text-accent hover:opacity-80 underline">pricing</a> ·
      <a href="/docs/mcp" class="text-accent hover:opacity-80 underline">MCP setup</a>. Measured 2026-10-11.
    </p>
  </section>

  <script is:inline>
    // perclient_1110 page-view beacon. Plain inline script, no imports
    // (define:vars/inline-script trap). /api/pv ships in a separate API PR and
    // may 404 for a while; nothing here may ever break the page.
    (function () {
      try {
        var p = new URLSearchParams(location.search);
        navigator.sendBeacon('/api/pv', JSON.stringify({
          path: location.pathname,
          utm_source: p.get('utm_source'),
          utm_medium: p.get('utm_medium'),
          utm_campaign: p.get('utm_campaign'),
          utm_content: p.get('utm_content'),
          referrer: document.referrer
        }));
      } catch (e) {}
      try {
        var btn = document.querySelector('[data-copy-prompt]');
        var pre = document.querySelector('[data-agent-prompt]');
        if (btn && pre && navigator.clipboard) {
          btn.addEventListener('click', function () {
            navigator.clipboard.writeText(pre.innerText).then(function () {
              btn.textContent = 'Copied';
              setTimeout(function () { btn.textContent = 'Copy'; }, 1500);
            }, function () {});
          });
        }
      } catch (e) {}
    })();
  </script>
</AppShell>
